.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt BSDEXTENSION 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BSDExtension
.Nd system-extension (kernel module) broker
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon owns loading of kernel
.Em system extensions
(kernel modules), a duty removed from
.Xr init 8
and
.Xr capsule 8 .
It is a socket-free capability-plane service: it exposes the well-known name
.Dq Li system.SystemExtension
and serves each client on its own
.Xr mac_capability 4
channel.
A client asks
.Nm
either to ensure a named extension is loaded or to query whether one is
currently loaded without attempting a load; the discovery
.Em domain
layer resolves
.Dq Li system.SystemExtension
only for SYSTEM-domain clients, so an ordinary user service can never reach
.Nm
and therefore can never cause kernel code to load.
.Pp
.Nm
is launched and supervised by
.Xr switchboard 8
from the
.Pa /Capabilities/System/SystemExtension.cap
bundle; it is not started by
.Xr init 8 ,
and it is not intended to be run from the command line.
.Ss Operation
During startup,
.Nm
declares the
.Dq kldload
system-capability gate in its manifest and
.Xr switchboard 8
mints the matching
.Dq system
token and delivers it as a bootstrap capability.
.Nm
holds no
.Pa /dev/mac_capability
handle of its own \(em capsule (PID 1) owns that device \(em and no socket
appears anywhere in the path;
.Fn service_provider_authorize_capabilities
adds
.Nm Ns 's
process nonce to the gate's authorized set, and it holds that authority for
its entire lifetime.
It then exposes its service name and dispatches each
accepted client on its own thread.
The broker is born in capability mode as the unprivileged capability user:
it performs each
.Xr kldload 2
.Em through
the held gate token in kernel context, so the raw privileged syscall stays
refused inside the Capsicum cage and the sandbox is never loosened.
Because a worker thread shares the daemon's held gate token, its
.Xr kldload 2
passes the gate under the standing claim; no access token is minted or
delivered, and no socket appears anywhere in the path.
.Pp
The protocol is the FIDL library
.Li bsd.extension.v1
.Pq Pa /usr/share/fidl/extensioncmp.fidl ;
a session calls
.Sy GetProtocolInfo
first.
Application errors are
.Xr errno 2
values; a method before
.Sy GetProtocolInfo ,
an unknown method, a malformed frame, a pre-FIDL record or an attached
descriptor closes the session without a reply.
.Pp
A request names a single kernel module as a safe filename component: a name
containing an embedded NUL or a slash, or equal to
containing a slash, or equal to
.Dq \&.
or
.Dq \&.. ,
is rejected.
For a load request
.Pq Sy Ensure
the module is loaded from the kernel module path with
.Xr kldload 2 ;
a module that is already present is reported as success, so the load path never
needs to query and uses only the
.Dq kldload
gate.
A query request
.Pq Sy Stat
instead reports whether the module is loaded, using
.Xr kldfind 2
for a module file and
.Xr modfind 2
for a module compiled into the kernel, both read-only queries; module
enumeration is ungated, so no capability is needed for it.
Both operations are subject to the same allow-list: a client may query only a
module it could load, so a name that is not allow-listed is refused rather than
answered, and a query leaks nothing about modules a client may not load.
.Pp
When the policy sets
.Cm device_drivers
to true, a module that is not allow-listed is also permitted if the running
kernel's
.Pa linker.hints
records its file with plug-and-play match data
.Pq Dv MDT_PNP_INFO ,
which makes it a hardware driver that
.Xr devmatch 8
can select for an unattached device, unless an administrator
.Cm deny
override names it.
.Nm
reads the hints beneath the running kernel's module directory (see
.Sx Kernel directory ) ,
bounded, and keeps them in memory while the file's device, inode, size,
modification and change times are unchanged, so a rebuilt
.Pa linker.hints
applies at the next request; without the directory, or while it no longer
holds the running kernel, the class permits nothing.
Service modules, filesystems and modules under
.Pa /boot/modules
need an allow-list entry.
.Pp
A client whose switchboard label the policy lists in
.Cm driver_clients
is admitted for the hardware-driver class only: it never loads or queries an
allow-listed module, and with
.Cm device_drivers
false it loads nothing.
The label comes from the requester's kernel-stamped authority, never from the
request.
The packaged policy lists
.Dq devd ,
the lasting grant
.Xr switchboard 8
gives
.Xr devd 8
.Pq Pa service-authority.ucl ,
so that a device attached after boot gets its driver through
.Xr devmatch 8 .
The grant follows devd into every program its images include, among them
.Pa /bin/sh ,
so every shell script a
.Xr devd.conf 5
rule runs holds it too; this class restriction, not the program list, is what
limits what those actions can load.
Each request is logged with its client label, for example
.Dq ENSURE if_urndis (client devd, hardware driver): loaded .
.Pp
.Nm
also serves
.Dq system.SystemExtension.Status ,
a read-only view over the
.Sy SystemExtensionStatus
protocol of the same
.Sy bsd.extension.v1
library.
It has only
.Sy GetProtocolInfo ,
.Sy Info ,
.Sy Next
and
.Sy NextDecision ;
a frame for any other method is an unknown ordinal and closes the session,
and the worker additionally decides nothing but
.Sy Info
and
.Sy Next
for a session that arrived on this name, whatever its rights.
.Xr switchboard 8
resolves it, besides for SYSTEM-domain callers, for UID 0 sessions that hold no
software grant, such as a root login shell
.Pq Pa root-query.ucl ;
.Xr sysextctl 8
.Cm status
uses it.
.Sy NextDecision
returns the recent load decisions, oldest first:
.Nm
keeps the 64 most recent
.Sy Ensure
and
.Sy Restore
outcomes in memory since it started, each with the time, module, client
label and result (loaded, already present, not permitted, client limited to
hardware drivers, administrator deny override, or the load error).
.Pp
INFO reports a class driver as allowed; boot activation
.Pq Cm enable
still needs an explicit
.Cm allow .
The full protocol has deliberately no unload operation; safe removal would
require explicit consumer release semantics; the service conservatively
retains session and boot claims until reboot.
The root
.Sy Unload
.Pq see Sx Root loads
is the one exception, limited to what a root load loaded.
.Pp
A
.Sy List
request enumerates the module allow-list, returning each permitted
module name and the count.
The allow-list is global rather than per-label, so every caller sees the same
set.
.Sy List
returns
.Er EOVERFLOW
above 32 permitted names.
.Sy Next
returns one entry after an exclusive lexical name cursor, supporting
bounded discovery of larger allow-lists.
.Pp
A
.Sy Reload
request requires ADMIN rights on the accepted service channel.
It rereads the shipped configuration through the delivered Config descriptor.
Administrator overrides are retained.
The replacement must be a regular file, not writable by group or others,
with an explicit allowed_extensions array.
Missing or invalid replacements leave the last active policy intact.
Updates are published atomically to all workers, including existing sessions.
Requests already admitted under the previous policy may finish.
Reload does not unload modules or restart the core service.
.Pp
.Sy Allow , Deny , Reset , Enable , Disable
and
.Sy Restore
also require ADMIN rights.
Permission overrides and desired boot activation are independent of the
shipped policy and stored in the private administrator configuration directory.
A successful edit syncs a replacement before publication.
Failure after rename but before directory sync fails closed until restart,
because durability cannot be established.
INFO and NEXT report permission, desired activation, and readiness; current
loaded state is reported only for permitted modules.
.Pp
The manifest supplies
.Pa /etc/bsdextension
as a directory descriptor.
The service reads administrator configuration before serving requests,
including bootstrap requests, without depending on the filesystem provider.
This supports UFS, ZFS, and read-only installer media.
A missing directory or malformed registry fails closed; an absent overrides
file in the delivered directory means no administrator overrides.
The late kld service restores boot activations after local filesystems mount.
These operations do not constitute package registration or authenticate
arbitrary module files; administrators remain responsible for installed code.
.Pp
.Nm
is a reconcile client of the capability container model
.Pq Pa docs/book/src/plane/containers-and-storage.md :
every module it loads on a bundle's behalf is attributed to that bundle
from the kernel-stamped container identity of the requesting unit, never
from the wire, in a per-boot owner map.
A forked reconcile child compares the map against the installed or running
bundles read from the delivered
.Pa /Capabilities/System ,
.Pa /Capabilities/Apps
and
.Pa /Capabilities/Run/live
directories: once at boot, then on a timer, unloading a bundle's module only
after it has been seen gone on two consecutive passes.
Only a module
.Nm
itself loaded is ever unloaded, and only when no other bundle still claims it;
a module found already loaded is attributed for the record but left alone,
and a module the kernel reports busy stays loaded and is retried on the next
pass.
The map is reset whenever the boot epoch changes, since modules do not
survive a reboot.
Reclaim is soft: without the map or the delivered directories,
.Nm
serves loads exactly as before.
.Ss Root loads
.Nm
serves a third name,
.Dq system.SystemExtension.RootLoad ,
over the
.Sy SystemExtensionRootLoad
protocol: only
.Sy GetProtocolInfo ,
.Sy Load
and
.Sy Unload .
A frame for any other method, Ensure and the policy methods included, is an
unknown ordinal and closes the session, and the full protocol's decision
refuses every request from a session that arrived on this name.
.Xr switchboard 8
resolves it for the same UID 0 sessions as the read-only view
.Pq Pa root-load.ucl ,
so that root can load a module of the base system from a login shell with
.Xr sysextctl 8 ,
while
.Xr kldload 2
from that shell stays refused by the gate.
.Pp
.Sy Load
does not consult the allow-list.
It refuses a protected module outright: a security policy
.Pq Pa mac_* ,
a tracing provider
.Pq DTrace, HyperTrace, kinst, fbt, sdt, systrace, fasttrap, profile ,
ZFS and opensolaris, a packet filter
.Pq Pa pf* , ipfw* ,
or vmm, the same list
.Sy Unload
refuses, with the result
.Dq protected module .
Before root loads existed the late-kldload gate refused all of them, and the
MAC policies among them grant privileges.
Otherwise it loads
.Ar NAME Ns Pa .ko
(the module name with
.Pa .ko
added when absent) from the running kernel's module directory, normally
.Pa /boot/kernel ,
only when the policy sets
.Cm root_load_repository ,
no administrator
.Cm deny
override names the module, and the module and every file the load would bring
in pass the checks below.
.Nm
resolves the module's dependencies
.Pq Dv MODULE_DEPEND
before anything is loaded, as the kernel linker would: from the module
metadata of each file, read by a forked child with
libkldelf
(below), a dependency that no loaded module provides is looked up in the
kernel directory's
.Pa linker.hints
(the record with the preferred version, else the highest version in range),
else as
.Ar dependency
or
.Ar dependency Ns Pa .ko
in that directory, and a file of the kernel itself
.Pq Pa kernel
needs nothing.
A dependency found nowhere in the kernel directory is refused; the kernel's
search of
.Va kern.module_path
.Pq for example Pa /boot/modules
is never used.
For the module and every such dependency:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the file, examined beneath the kernel directory without following symbolic
links, is a regular file; anything else is refused before it is opened, so a
device node or FIFO is never opened;
.It
the package database
.Pq Pa /var/db/pkg/local.sqlite ,
read through the delivered
.Pa /var/db/pkg
directory, records that path in a package whose origin starts with
.Dq base/
and whose
.Dq repository
annotation, which
.Xr pkg 8
writes at installation, equals
.Cm root_load_repository ;
.It
the file's BLAKE2b-512 checksum, in pkg's
.Dq 2$
encoding, equals the checksum pkg recorded for it;
.It
no administrator
.Cm deny
override names it and it is not a protected module;
.It
a dependency provides the version range the file that needs it declares.
.El
.Pp
The files are then loaded dependencies first, each by its absolute path in the
kernel directory, so the kernel resolves nothing itself.
After each load
.Nm
compares the loaded files with those before it: any file other than the one
just loaded, or a file id whose pathname
.Xr kldstat 2
cannot report, is a hard failure, logged at
.Dv LOG_ERR .
Every file this request loaded is then unloaded again and the load is
refused, as it is when a file changed (device, inode, size, modification or
change time) or the kernel directory changed between the check and the end of
the load.
At most 16 files are loaded for one request.
Dependencies the load brought in are root loads in their own right: each can
be unloaded with
.Sy Unload ,
the module that needs them first.
A module already loaded or compiled into the kernel is reported as present
without a check.
.Pp
The package database and module metadata are not parsed in the broker: a
.Xr pdfork 2 Ns ed
child, which has no kldload token (it is close-on-fork) and closes every
descriptor but the file or directory it reads and its reply pipe, does it and
returns one fixed-size record, which the broker validates again.
The database child maps
.Pa local.sqlite
privately and reads it with base's private sqlite library through
.Fn sqlite3_deserialize
read-only, with views, triggers and untrusted schema functions disabled.
.Nm
is threaded; the child runs only the forking thread, and libc's allocator and
stdio have fork handlers, so the child may allocate.
.Pp
.Sy Unload
unloads only a module that a root
.Sy Load
loaded since
.Nm
started, through the held kldunload gate, and only while its file id still
names that file.
It refuses once any other client has asked for the module
.Pq an Sy Ensure No that found it present ,
and never unloads a protected module.
The kernel refuses a module that is busy or that another module depends on.
.Pp
Each root request is recorded in the decision list with the client
.Dq root (uid 0)
and operation
.Sy Load
(14) or
.Sy Unload
(15), and logged with the switchboard label, the requester's program nonce,
the result and the reason, for example
.Dq ROOT LOAD x (uid 0 session, ...) -> DENIED (not a file of a 5BSD base package); no installed package records /boot/kernel/x.ko .
The kernel stamp carries no login session or process id;
.Xr sysextctl 8
logs those, with the audit identifiers, on its side.
The results are: loaded, already present, administrator deny override,
failed (with the errno), not a file of a 5BSD base package (7), checksum
differs from the package record (8), no regular file in the kernel directory,
a dependency outside it, or a kernel directory that no longer holds the
running kernel (9), unloaded (10), not loaded by a root load (11), root loads
not configured (12), package database unavailable (13), a file changed or an
unchecked file was loaded during the load (14), protected module (15).
.Pp
Who reaches the endpoint is
.Xr switchboard 8 Ns 's
decision: a session whose kernel-stamped effective uid is 0, outside a jail,
holding no software authority.
The stamp carries no real uid, so a set-user-ID-root program run by another
user counts as uid 0 here, as it does for the read-only view.
Any process running as root without a token qualifies, not only a login
shell: for example
.Xr devd 8
restarted from a root shell, which then has no lasting grant, still reaches
.Sy Load
for any base module that passes these checks, so the program limit of devd's
lasting grant
.Pq Xr switchboard 8
does not bound what root can load this way.
.Pp
This is a guard against mistakes and an audit trail, not containment of root.
The package database and
.Pa /boot
are writable by root: a root that edits the database, swaps a file between
the check and the load, replaces or mounts over
.Pa /boot ,
installs a package, or edits
.Xr loader.conf 5
and reboots, defeats it.
A dependency that is already loaded is accepted by module name
.Pq Xr modfind 2 ;
its version is not visible to
.Nm ,
and when it does not satisfy the module the kernel searches
.Va kern.module_path
itself; the file it loads is detected and unloaded, but its initialisation
has run.
The capability plane isolates
.Va kern.module_path
.Pq Xr BSDSysctl 8 ,
so that search stays within the loader's directories.
.Xr mac_veriexec 4
is the control against a compromised root.
.Ss Kernel directory
The manifest delivers
.Pa /boot
by descriptor.
The kernel directory is the one the loader booted the running kernel from:
the pathname
.Xr kldstat 2
reports for file id 1
.Pq Pa /boot/kernel/kernel , No or for example Pa /boot/kernel.old/kernel ,
which nothing can change after boot
.Po
.Va kern.bootfile
is a writable string and is not used
.Pc .
It must be a single directory beneath
.Pa /boot ,
not a symbolic link, and its kernel file must carry the running kernel's GNU
build-id
.Pq Va kern.build_id .
Booted from
.Pa /boot/kernel.old ,
the hardware-driver class reads that directory's
.Pa linker.hints ;
root loads find no package records there and are refused.
.Pp
Before each driver-class decision and before and after every root load,
.Nm
opens the directory again by name beneath
.Pa /boot
and compares it with the descriptor it holds:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the same directory (device and inode) whose kernel file still has the running
build-id is used;
.It
a different directory under the name, as after
.Cm installkernel
renamed the old one to
.Pa kernel.old ,
or after a mount over it, is adopted, and logged, only when its kernel file
has the running build-id;
.It
otherwise, and when the kernel file was replaced in place (a kernel package
upgrade before the reboot), driver and root loads are refused and logged until
the next boot: the directory's modules belong to another kernel.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width BSDEXTENSION_RECLAIM_INTERVAL
.It Ev BSDEXTENSION_RECLAIM_INTERVAL
Seconds between the timer passes of the module reconcile, and therefore the
grace window an uninstalled bundle's modules are given before they are
unloaded.
Accepted range 10 to 86400; the default is 300.
Set through the unit's
.Ic environment
block.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /Capabilities/System/SystemExtension.cap" -compact
.It Pa /Capabilities/System/SystemExtension.cap
capability bundle shipping
.Nm
.It Pa /Capabilities/System/SystemExtension.cap/Units/bsdextension.unit/Config/bsdextension.ucl
Required allow-list policy, read at daemon startup and on authorized reload.
The allowed_extensions array specifies the complete base list of named
modules; the optional boolean device_drivers enables the hardware-driver
class described above; the optional driver_clients array names up to eight
client labels limited to that class; the optional root_load_repository string
names the pkg repository whose base packages root loads accept (shipped:
.Dq 5BSD-base ;
absent refuses root loads).
The executable contains no default module grants.
Missing or malformed startup policy is fatal; a failed reload preserves the
last accepted policy.
An empty array, with device_drivers absent or false, denies all module
requests.
Both base policy and persisted overrides reject includes/macros, duplicate
keys, unknown fields, embedded NULs and implicit arrays. Reads are bounded to
one MiB and do not consume or reposition the caller's descriptor.
The packaged configuration selects the base system's on-demand modules.
The CPU telemetry drivers are built into the amd64 and i386 GENERIC kernels;
the allow-list also supports loading them on kernels that omit them.
Graphics startup uses this broker when the capability plane is enabled.
Firmware requests made during driver attachment retain the securelevel check;
the firmware-load privilege is available to an authorized module-loading gate
holder, and packaged GPU firmware is loaded as data from
.Pa /boot/firmware .
Use
.Xr sysextctl 8
to manage persistent permission and activation overrides, or reload defaults.
.It Pa /boot/kernel/linker.hints
kernel module hints, read for the device_drivers class
.It Pa /var/db/pkg/local.sqlite
the package database root loads check, read by a forked child
.It Pa /etc/bsdextension/overrides.ucl
Persistent administrator overrides, managed through sysextctl.
.It Pa /var/run/BSDExtension/modules.meta
The per-boot module to bundle owner map the reconcile works from.
.El
.Sh DTRACE PROBES
The
.Dq BSDExtension
provider exports
.Sy request-done
with the historical operation code
.Pq 0 for negotiation ,
the status and the transport error, and
.Sy list ,
which fires when a
.Sy List
request is answered with the client label, the number
of allow-listed modules reported, and the result.
The result argument is zero on success or an
.Xr errno 2
value on failure.
.Pp
.Sy reclaim_pass
fires after every reconcile pass with when (0 boot, 1 timer) and the pass's
live, owned, orphaned, destroyed and failed counts.
